    <td>
<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>Origin - UWOHoC Chat for 8/30/2000</B></font>
		<p></p>

<font face=arial size=-1><br><b>&lt;Cynthe&gt;</b> Hi everyone, and welcome to the second House of Commons for UWO:ORIGIN!
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Greetings and salutations folks.  Welcome to our next chat.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> The topic is open tonight, so feel free to start slinging some questions at us.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> &lt;Thor_Anderson&gt; Will unarmored combat be a viable solution for fighters? In terms of learning defensive moves to dodge blows, instead of wearing armor to absorb blows?
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> I just read on the BB's that you were going to ask that question, Thor....
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> let me see if I can tackle that...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> Yes, there are skills that will enable you to increase your defenses...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> This includes skills that make you harder to hit, take less damage, avoid stun, etc...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> So it is very viable to elect to spend your skill points on these skills over armor...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> Also, because heavier armors do impose penalties to evasion...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> You may very well want to stay away from them to maximize your defense.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b>  *Vernes* Will we be able to subdue players? as in, not killing them but putting them KO? And can we drag there bodies out of our houses? :)
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> Yes indeedy.  Generally speaking, you will always fall unconscious before you die.
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> This includes PvP combat...
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> So you will be able to subdue someone for a bit without needing to actually kill them.  And this means you can spar with your buddies easily.
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> As for dragging, still under discussion :)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Spiro* Thus far I have seen very little in the way of magic. Especially in the most wonderful movies we've been seeing. How will magic be delt with without a use base system
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Well, there's a good reason why you haven't seen much magic in the movies yet -
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Our particle system and primary magic system is currently in the stage of implementation now, so hopefully that means good stuff to show Real Soon Now.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> As for the 'use-based' system, our skill tree system offers greater flexibility as far as how you can pump your skills
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Do you want more powerful spells?  Spells with longer durations?  Shorter trance times?  Faster recharge times?
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> The skill trees allow mages to choose the skills that best create the character they want to be, as well as the character best suited for their play style.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> So I think you'll be okay. =)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b>  *Epoch* ? Will there be a program similiar to the Seers in UWO/UO2
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Most likely, yes.  We are working on new ways to harness the volunteer powers to better tell the story we want to tell.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Origin will be a world with a strong sense of history and forward progress of the fiction.  That only happens if we make a concerted effort to present that story to you, so it touches your lives.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Tyrant is working to find the best way to leverage the volunteer workforce, and I have supreme confidence that we will have a good plan by launch.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Tyrant r0xx0rs
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> I heard he's unfair to some.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> and whatnot
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *[EKC]Brucie* Will more of the changes to the shards take place while the shard is active rather than having to shut them down to update them?
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Because of the way we're data-driven, we can do a lot of changes without shard outage, or with minimal outage
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We have a pretty powerful database on the back end which handles lots o that stuff for us in the background
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> However, into every shard, some maintenance downtime must fall - but we should be pretty damn good for the industry
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We can do lots of client updates via patching which are normally tough to do as well
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> So all is good on the extensibiltiy front - we've put lots of dev effort into it.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> The game will only get more fun in the years to come, without being too constrained by architecture.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b>  *dark_chro* "What would you say the Current progress is, can i get a percent?
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Heh.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Pi%
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> We're about 90% done.  Now we just have to do the other 90%.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We have done tons of architecture work -
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> In all seriousness, we've done almost all of the extraordinarily hard stuff, which is the architecture the game rests on.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> this bears lots of fruit as individual systems come online
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> We're mostly at this point filling the world with content - and it takes a massive amount of content to fill one of these games
<br><b>&lt;Vaxhacker&gt;</b> and that's good, because we like fruit.  especially bananas.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Otherwise you all get bored.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *[EKC]Brucie* Will there be any chance of seeing shards that are designed for specific groups of players as well as shards for different physical locations?
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> I assume you mean shards with different rules, a la Siege Perilous.  
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> The problems with these shards is that they are seperate code paths, and this can quickly turn into a process, support, and implementation nightmare
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> As all of your additions, patches, and fixes have to work on all servers with all rulesets.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> This is not to discount the possibility of adding additional server types with additional rulesets after launch
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> But for right now, we're concentrating on getting UWO:Origin Prime done. =)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Fitch* So far there hasn't been anything solid said on the amount of characters we will get per account. Has it been decided how many we'll get yet? if so or not, why?
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Character slots is a tricky question - we're not decided yet, and there are a few reasons behind this.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We'd like to have a small number of slots for a few reasons:
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Less anonymous chars, less muling of skills that other players could hold roles for, etc.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> However, on the big slot side, we don't want to annoy people that are used to mondo numbers of chars unnecissarily
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Er I can spell
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> So...that's why it's undecided.  I happen to think lower slot numbers make for a more fun game...but I agree with the reasons against.  We'll probably take this questions to focus groups and fans for an answer.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Dunfore* Will Origin require high powered 3d cards (ex. 128meg)? What will be the demand on ram that the game has?
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> In a word, Dunfore, nope - we will run on pretty low end cards
<br><b>&lt;SkiCat&gt;</b> We have tried very hard in the design to support a very wide range of cards
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We are targetting 8meg cards, though targets have been known to change by ship (we're not dopes, but it's sometimes tough to judge where the market will be in the future)
<br><b>&lt;SkiCat&gt;</b> But, to get all the really cool fancy effects you might need a pretty high-end card
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> For ram, we're aiming at 64 meg boxes if I recall correctly.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> That's regular ram, not vram.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Incidentally, everyone pretend you can say hi to Skicat, one of our graphics programmers.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Again, your mileage may vary by ship - we're trying to keep it as low as possible while still having drop-your-jaw gameplay and graphics
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Aye, he's Mr. Terrain and Water, amongst other things
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Cavern* I've heard UWO being labelled a 3rd generation game over and over again by different reviewers. What does UWO have that gives it this distinction? How is it an improvement over 2nd generation games that are STILL in development like Anarchy Online and Shadowbane?
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Well, calling us 3rd generation in itself depends on where you start counting.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> I'm guessing the author of that statement considered Meridian, the Realm, and DragonRealms to be 1st generation
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> And EQ, AC and UO to be second generation.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Which would put us in the 3rd generation with SB and AO.
<br><b>&lt;Cynthe&gt;</b> And if you start counting at Pong, we're 5,872,694th generation
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Of course that leaves out the 0th generations of MUDs...
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> BUt of course, the history of MMPRPGs goes back much farther than that - nearly 20 years
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Back to Richard Bartle's first MUD.  And some say it goes back even farther.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Fortunately, there are a lot of historical lessons to learn from all that knowledge - but I digress.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> When the first cavemen used PDP-11s
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> HOw will we be better than SB or AO?  I don't know if I would say better (Okay, I will, but then, it's my damn game!)
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Lol Ubiq
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> Proud are you.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> But I will say that we are putting a lot of emphasis on economy, on adventuring, on story, and on extreme depth of character customization and skills
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> That I think will make UWO:O stand extremely strongly.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> I'd add that we have very solid, stable, 3rd generation style tech as well - we've learned a lot from our earlier games
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Caperon|NEX* What is the status on pvp... In a true role playing enviroment there is player vs player conflict... If someone does something he is not supposed to. What can the other players threaten? A slap on the wrist? How about a pvp server. Guild warfare?...
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> PvP: Safe lands, there isn't any.  It will be similar to EQ and Asheron's Call in that respect, so you can play without being pestered.  In the Wild lands:
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> Although we currently plan to restrictions on who may attack whom, we have a pretty good tracking system to display who is a violent type and who is not...and we have a few systems planned to make life more difficult for the violent.
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> And with the ability to knock someone out without killing them, consensual warfare is easy :)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *{UGS}Fyren[AoTWC]* Lately we have been given quite a bit of information regarding NPC guilds and clans. How will these guilds/clans play a part in UWOO? Will we be able to join them?
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> Because our skill trees are data-driven, we can add new skills and titles on the fly...
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> We have taken some of our fictionaly guilds and clans and implemented those into the skill trees so you can learn skills and gain titles of the same groups that are in the fiction.
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> These are the higher-end skills, of course - if you want to be a Scirocco Assassin, you have to know your stuff.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> (have we mentioned yet that skill trees are really, really cool?)
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> And we can add more if people ask for them, too :)...easily.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> (REALLY cool)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b>  *GoldenDragon* Please tell us about the communications system in Origins and will voice chat be possible?
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> We will have a number of ways to communicate - some are in place, some are still being worked on
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> R0xx0r, this is Kewlio, Over.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> you can talk amongst your group, in an area, and a variety of other ways
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> No voice chat in the game (believe me, you wouldn't want to have to pay for the extra bandwidth)
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> But...you can certainly use the other voice internet programs alongside our game
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Which gives a lot of what you'd want from that.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Warrion|UH* We know that you can customize your character all sorts of ways with clothing, dyes, hair, etc. but what about while wearing armor? Will you as a male meer in pneumatic armor look like every other male meer in pneumatic armor or are there ways to customize your look further?
