<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *EternalNewbie* Will magic users only be allowed to advance through fighting, or can they advance by study of magic?
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> We have several plans in place to ensure that non-combatant skills (especially craftsmen and resource harvesters) have ways to get experience
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> And some of these will extend to non-craftsmen as well, such as magicians
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> However, we expect most of the fighting crafts to get the bulk of their experience through fighting monsters
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> And magic users are, predominantly, a fighting class.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> So to answer your question, they will have some non-combat means to get experiencem
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> but the design team isn't putting as much emphasis on solving that problem as they are for the craftsmen.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Evil_Elvis* Question: How strong is it possible for a character to become in terms of the strenght of a monster.. IE: is becoming strong enough to solo most monsters at high levels feasible?
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> We definately want to make it possible for characters to solo hunt...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> Keep in mind however, that hunting solo make take a little more work than group hunting..
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> Furthermore, there will be a handful of uber critters that will probably be extremely difficult to solo...
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> I don't say impossible, because I'm sure someone will figure out how. :)
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b> *Merwyndin* How about magical weapons? Will they use the same system as in UO, will they be rarer?
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> The magic system for item enchantments is very different from UO's
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Fundamentally, our 'magic item' system is geared towards allowing players to craft the magic goodies themselves.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Aye, you can add lots of cool stuff - it's very dynamic
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> After all, craftsmen are a lot less important to your economy if they are made obsolete by the loot that monsters drop.
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> Allowing craftsmen to make magic items is very central to our concept of the Adventurer's Economy.
<br><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b> We're also gearing monster treasure towards dropping things that can be used to make rare stuff.
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> There'll be critters you want to hunt because of a resource they provide...
<br><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b> How rare will they be?  Well, that would depend on how rare the resources required to make them are.
<br><b>&lt;Gaffer&gt;</b> Part of the same systems that let our game be extensible to GMs puts lots of power in the players' hands as well - by in this case allowing for complex enchantments to be placed on individual items
<br><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b> I suspect everyone will be pouncing on those poor Trolls for the healing properties of their blood.
<br>* Ubiq mourns for the poor, misunderstood trolls.
<br>* Gaffer pounces on Ubiq for the healing quality of his blood.
<p><b>&lt;Joshua_Rowan&gt;</b>  *Menelian* Hi, I haven't followed the development of owo:origin as some of the people in here I'm sure, but in the first trailer released, a character does a front flip over another character...is this going to be an actual fighting move or was it just for promotional purposes?
